    <div class="starter-callout">
      <span class="badge">🔥 Starter</span>
      <div>
        <h3>The Tin Can Telephone</h3>
        <p>A tin can telephone allows one person to transmit a message in binary by saying 1 or 0.</p>
        <p><strong>How is it possible for both people to send a message to each other?</strong></p>
        <p><strong>How could messages be sent faster?</strong></p>
        <p><em>Discuss with your partner — be ready to share your ideas.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Learning Objectives -->
    <div class="objectives-box">
      <strong><i class="fas fa-bullseye" style="color:#1f6390; margin-right:6px;"></i> Learning Objectives:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Describe how data is transmitted using <strong>serial</strong> and <strong>parallel</strong> methods.</li>
        <li>Explain the suitability, advantages and disadvantages of each method.</li>
        <li>Describe <strong>simplex</strong>, <strong>half-duplex</strong> and <strong>full-duplex</strong> transmission.</li>
        <li>Understand the <strong>USB</strong> interface and explain how it is used to transmit data.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <!-- Why this matters -->
    <div class="why-matters-box">
      <span class="badge">💡 Why This Matters</span>
      <div>
        <h3>From Tin Cans to Terabits</h3>
        <p>The tin can telephone is a <strong>half-duplex</strong> system — both people can send messages, but only one at a time. Modern networks have evolved far beyond this, but the same fundamental ideas still apply. Understanding the difference between serial and parallel, and between simplex, half-duplex, and full-duplex, helps you understand why USB replaced older cables, why your internet connection works the way it does, and why a modern CPU can communicate with RAM at incredible speeds.</p>
      </div>
