        <p>Read the information below and answer the questions. You must answer <strong>all 4</strong> before moving on.</p>

        <div class="timer-hint-box">
          <i class="fas fa-clock"></i>
          <span>⏱️ Click the timer above to start. The next task unlocks automatically when time is up. Or click the yellow "Complete Task" button if you finish early.</span>
        </div>

        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-arrow-right" style="color:#2a6f97;"></i> Serial Transmission</h4>
            <p>Bits are sent <strong>one signal at a time</strong> over a single wire.</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="pro">✔ Only one wire needed — cheaper and smaller cables</li>
              <li class="pro">✔ Can be used over longer distances without crosstalk or skew</li>
              <li class="pro">✔ Modern processors are fast enough to handle high serial speeds</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Historically slower than parallel (but no longer true)</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Examples:</strong> USB, SATA, Ethernet, broadband internet</p>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-bars" style="color:#2a6f97;"></i> Parallel Transmission</h4>
            <p><strong>Multiple wires</strong> are used so that many bits may be sent at the same time.</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="pro">✔ Faster than serial if the same data rate is used per wire</li>
              <li class="pro">✔ Still used inside computers (CPU ↔ RAM)</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Suffers from <strong>crosstalk</strong> (signals interfering)</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Suffers from <strong>skew</strong> (signals arriving at slightly different times)</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Requires many more wires — thicker, more expensive cables</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Examples:</strong> Older PATA/IDE hard drive cables, parallel printer cables</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="highlight-box amber">
          <i class="fas fa-exclamation-triangle" style="color:#b45309; margin-right:8px;"></i>
          <strong>Key point:</strong> Parallel cables were common before the year 2000, but they have been replaced by <strong>SATA</strong> (Serial ATA) and <strong>USB</strong> (Universal Serial Bus) cables. Why? Because serial transmission avoids the problems of crosstalk and skew, and as processors became faster, serial transmission became the faster option.
        </div>

