      <div class="teacher-answer" id="answer_sc2">
        <strong>Suggested answer:</strong><br>
        Parallel cables suffer from <strong>crosstalk</strong> and <strong>skew</strong>, which get worse over longer distances. Before the year 2000, processors were not fast enough to keep up with very fast serial transmission, so parallel was used to increase overall speed. As processors became faster, serial transmission could achieve higher speeds without crosstalk or skew. Serial cables also require far fewer wires, making them cheaper to make and smaller, and they allow for longer cables without the risks of crosstalk and skew.
