        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-arrow-right" style="color:#2a6f97;"></i> Simplex</h4>
            <p>Data travels in <strong>one direction only</strong>.</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="pro">✔ Simple and cheap</li>
              <li class="pro">✔ Entire bandwidth used for one direction</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Cannot send and receive</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Keyboard → computer</p>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-arrows-alt-h" style="color:#2a6f97;"></i> Half-Duplex</h4>
            <p>Data can travel in <strong>both directions</strong> along a single connection, but <strong>not at the same time</strong>.</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="pro">✔ Both devices can send and receive</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Cannot send and receive simultaneously</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Walkie talkies</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-card">
            <h4><i class="fas fa-arrows-alt-h" style="color:#2a6f97;"></i> Full-Duplex</h4>
            <p>Data can travel in <strong>both directions simultaneously</strong> using two cables (or two fibres).</p>
            <ul>
              <li class="pro">✔ Fastest — can send and receive at the same time</li>
              <li class="pro">✔ Full bandwidth in both directions</li>
              <li class="con">✘ Requires more cables/infrastructure</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> Ethernet network cables, fibre optic cables</p>
          </div>
